-- #1265 CONTROL — the BOUNDARY of the defect, flipping exactly ONE dimension.
--
-- The two interfaces still have DIFFERENT names and still share the METHOD name
-- `speak`; both impls are still METHOD-LESS and still cross-module.  The ONE thing
-- that changes is that they are implemented on TWO types (`KDog`, `KCat`) instead of
-- one, so the two defaults land at two different dispatch TAGS.
--
-- That is precisely the condition #1264's narrowing can handle: `ifaceIdsAtTag "KDog"`
-- returns one identity, `narrowDefaults` has exactly one survivor, and the right body
-- is selected.  So this control proves the defect is the SHARED TAG, not the shared
-- method name and not method-less impls in general — and it must stay GREEN under any
-- fix for #1265.
--
-- Per #1144 this harness grades a control on its EXIT CODE ONLY, and a
-- wrong-value-at-exit-0 bug cannot be discriminated by an exit code unless the program
-- puts the value INTO the exit code.  So `main` ASSERTS the correct string and panics
-- otherwise, rather than printing it — a control that merely printed would exit 0
-- either way and could never fail.
import da.{KDog(..), sayKA}
import db.{KCat(..), sayKB}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let r = sayKA KDog ++ "|" ++ sayKB KCat
  if r == "A-default|B-default" then
    println "control-ok:A-default|B-default"
  else
    panic "control regressed: with the two interfaces on SEPARATE types the defaults no longer resolve to A-default|B-default"
